1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,416
Today I want to tell you about one of my favorite stories in mathematics:

2
00:00:04,416 --> 00:00:05,431
the Basel problem.

3
00:00:05,431 --> 00:00:08,306
A sum that resisted solution for eighty-five years,

4
00:00:08,306 --> 00:00:12,760
before a young mathematician finally cracked it with a beautifully simple idea.

5
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,274
Here's the question.

6
00:00:15,274 --> 00:00:21,569
Take every perfect square — one, four, nine, sixteen, twenty-five, and so on —

7
00:00:21,569 --> 00:00:24,797
flip each one over, and add them all up.

8
00:00:24,797 --> 00:00:29,800
Does this infinite sum converge? And if it does, then to what?

9
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:35,180
The problem was first posed by Pietro Mengoli in 1650.

10
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:37,048
The sum does converge —

11
00:00:37,048 --> 00:00:42,027
you can check that by comparison with the integral of one over x squared.

12
00:00:42,027 --> 00:00:45,913
Add the first thousand terms and you get about... 1.6439.

13
00:00:45,913 --> 00:00:50,619
It clearly approaches something near 1.6449. But what is this number?

14
00:00:50,619 --> 00:00:52,460
Nobody could figure it out.

15
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:59,040
Even Jakob Bernoulli, one of the greatest mathematicians of his era,

16
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:00,975
admitted defeat in 1689.

17
00:01:00,975 --> 00:01:01,700
He wrote:

18
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:05,784
If anyone finds, and communicates to us,

19
00:01:05,784 --> 00:01:11,960
that which thus far has eluded our efforts — great will be our gratitude.

20
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:16,365
For eighty-five years, nobody could solve it.

21
00:01:16,365 --> 00:01:20,698
Then in 1734, a twenty-seven year old named Leonhard Euler,

22
00:01:20,698 --> 00:01:26,060
working at the Academy of Sciences in Saint Petersburg, found the answer.

23
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:31,038
The sum equals pi squared over six. And that was shocking.

24
00:01:31,038 --> 00:01:35,116
Pi is the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter —

25
00:01:35,116 --> 00:01:39,194
why on earth would it show up in a sum of reciprocal squares?

26
00:01:39,194 --> 00:01:41,400
Let me show you Euler's argument.

27
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,782
Euler's proof starts with something you might not expect — the sine function.

28
00:01:47,782 --> 00:01:53,201
We know the Taylor series for sine of x: x, minus x cubed over three factorial,

29
00:01:53,201 --> 00:01:56,700
plus x to the fifth over five factorial, and so on.

30
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:02,788
For any fixed nonzero x, divide that series by x.

31
00:02:02,788 --> 00:02:08,997
We get sine of x over x equals one, minus x squared over six,

32
00:02:08,997 --> 00:02:11,440
plus higher-order terms.

33
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:17,107
Hold onto that coefficient of x squared — it's negative one sixth.

34
00:02:17,107 --> 00:02:19,460
That number is going to come back.

35
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,588
Now here's Euler's bold move.

36
00:02:22,588 --> 00:02:28,700
The function sine of x over x equals zero whenever x is plus or minus pi,

37
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,220
plus or minus two pi, plus or minus three pi, and so on.

38
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:36,158
Euler reasoned by analogy.

39
00:02:36,158 --> 00:02:39,692
Just as a polynomial can be factored by its roots,

40
00:02:39,692 --> 00:02:43,156
he wrote sine of x over x as an infinite product,

41
00:02:43,156 --> 00:02:45,559
one factor for each pair of roots.

42
00:02:45,559 --> 00:02:50,436
This was an assumption, not a proof — the rigorous justification came

43
00:02:50,436 --> 00:02:54,960
over a century later with the Weierstrass factorization theorem.

44
00:02:56,060 --> 00:03:00,690
Before the infinite product, see why this works for a finite one.

45
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:02,044
Take three factors.

46
00:03:02,044 --> 00:03:07,102
An x squared term only appears when you pick the negative a-k x squared

47
00:03:07,102 --> 00:03:11,875
piece from exactly one factor, and the one from every other factor.

48
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:17,218
So the x squared coefficient is just the sum a-one plus a-two plus a-three,

49
00:03:17,218 --> 00:03:18,500
with a minus sign.

50
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:21,972
Extend this to the infinite product.

51
00:03:21,972 --> 00:03:27,111
By the same reasoning, the x squared coefficient is minus one over pi squared,

52
00:03:27,111 --> 00:03:28,560
times our mystery sum.

53
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:30,735
Now we compare.

54
00:03:30,735 --> 00:03:36,040
The Taylor series told us the x squared coefficient is negative one sixth.

55
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:42,219
And the infinite product told us it's negative one over pi squared,

56
00:03:42,219 --> 00:03:43,280
times our sum.

57
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:50,085
Both are power series for the same function, and a power series is unique.

58
00:03:50,085 --> 00:03:52,474
So the coefficients must match.

59
00:03:52,474 --> 00:03:56,560
One over pi squared, times the sum, equals one sixth.

60
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:01,679
Multiply both sides by pi squared, and there it is.

61
00:04:01,679 --> 00:04:06,880
One plus one fourth plus one ninth plus one sixteenth, on forever,

62
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:09,008
equals pi squared over six.

63
00:04:09,008 --> 00:04:14,682
That mysterious 1.6449 that nobody could pin down for eighty-five years.

64
00:04:14,682 --> 00:04:18,780
The circle constant was hiding in the sum all along.

65
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:23,408
Should we worry that Euler cut corners? Mathematicians did.

66
00:04:23,408 --> 00:04:28,274
It took over a century before Weierstrass made the factoring step rigorous.

67
00:04:28,274 --> 00:04:33,140
But the answer was never in doubt. Sometimes intuition runs ahead of proof.

68
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:39,074
What we computed is a special value of the Riemann zeta function,

69
00:04:39,074 --> 00:04:40,264
at s equals two.

70
00:04:40,264 --> 00:04:45,619
Euler showed all the even values are rational multiples of powers of pi.

71
00:04:45,619 --> 00:04:51,420
But the odd values? Zeta of three, zeta of five? Still mysterious to this day.

72
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:53,810
So what did we learn?

73
00:04:53,810 --> 00:04:57,494
A simple question can hide an answer of extraordinary depth.

74
00:04:57,494 --> 00:05:02,100
Euler's genius was the boldness to try something that had no right to work,

75
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:04,679
and the taste to see beauty in the result.

76
00:05:04,679 --> 00:05:09,100
And at the heart of it, pi, quietly connecting circles to whole numbers.
